        rules: {
          objective: 'Avoid having the most points OR the least points in a round.',
          gameplay: '1. Must follow suit if possible.\n2. Highest card of led suit wins the trick.\n3. The winner of a trick leads the next.',
          keyRules: [
            'Must follow suit if possible.',
            'Highest card of the led suit wins the trick.',
            'Trick winner leads the next trick.',
            'Avoid having the most or the least points in a round; middle score survives.',
            'Last trick carries a 3-point penalty.',
            'High and low scorers lose one life point; last player with life wins.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Take exactly one trick. Once you have captured one trick containing a low point card (like a King or a 3), stop winning. Leading small cards (4s and 5s) for the rest of the game is the safest way to stay in the 'middle' bracket.",
          intermediate: 'Dump your Aces. Because an Ace is worth 3 points, it is a dangerous liability. Try to play your Aces early into tricks led by others, especially if you cannot win the trick.',
          advanced: "The Outlier Trap: If you see a player has 0 points and it's near the end, lead a suit they *must* follow to force them to win a trick. This protects you from being the 'low' loser and forces them into the game.",
          tips: [
            'Last Trick: Avoid winning the last trick at all costs, as it carries a 3-point penalty (plus whatever cards are in it).',
            "Suit Balance: If you have a long suit of 4-5-6, keep it for the end of the game to ensure you don't take unwanted tricks.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
